-- Q2 [Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins', 'A researcher studying a newly characterized hydrolase finds that the enzyme is catalytically inactive unless a Zn2+ ion is bound within its active site, where the metal helps stabilize a developing negative charge during the reaction. Based on the chemical nature of this required Zn2+ ion, it is best classified as which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A coenzyme"}, {"label": "B", "text": "An inorganic cofactor"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A prosthetic group"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A substrate"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because cofactors required for catalysis are divided into two categories—metals and coenzymes—and a metal ion such as Zn2+ is the inorganic (metal) type of cofactor. The text states, ''Cofactors are molecules that bind to enzymes and are required for catalytic activity. They can be divided into two major categories: metals and coenzymes'' (lines 32720-32721). It further specifies, ''Metal cofactors commonly found in human enzymes include iron, magnesium, manganese, cobalt, copper, zinc, and molybdenum'' (lines 32721-32722), so a required Zn2+ ion is an inorganic metal cofactor. (Choice A) is incorrect: coenzymes are the organic class of cofactor—the text defines them as ''small organic molecules that are often derived from vitamins'' (line 32722)—whereas a Zn2+ ion is a metal, not an organic molecule. (Choice B) is correct: a Zn2+ ion is a metal, and metals are explicitly named as one of the two cofactor categories, with zinc listed among the metal cofactors (lines 32721-32722). (Choice C) is incorrect: a prosthetic group is a tightly bound organic coenzyme, not a metal classification—the text states, ''Tight-binding coenzymes are referred to as prosthetic groups'' (line 32727); even if the Zn2+ were tightly bound, the chemical-nature classification asked here is the inorganic-metal category. (Choice D) is incorrect: a substrate is a molecule transformed into product during the reaction (e.g., ''by NADH (the substrate) to form lactic acid and NAD+ (the products)'', line 21278), whereas the Zn2+ is a required cofactor that assists catalysis rather than being consumed as a reactant. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that cofactors are split into inorganic metal cofactors and organic coenzymes, and that a required metal ion such as Zn2+ falls into the inorganic cofactor category.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
