-- Q3 [Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins', 'A clinician is counseling a patient about diet and notes that one vitamin must be replenished through regular intake because, unlike the isoprenoid-derived vitamins that partition into and are retained by the body''s lipid stores, this vitamin is water-soluble and excess amounts are not retained. Which vitamin is the clinician most likely describing?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Vitamin A"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Vitamin D"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Vitamin C (ascorbic acid)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Vitamin K"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because vitamin C is the only listed vitamin that is water-soluble; the textbook explicitly identifies it as such, stating that ''Ascorbic acid, a water-soluble vitamin and antioxidant/reducing agent'' is added to liposomes. Because a water-soluble vitamin does not partition into the body''s lipid (fat) stores, excess amounts are not retained, so it must be replenished regularly through the diet. The other three options are all members of the fat-soluble, isoprenoid-derived group: the source notes that the figure ''shows other biologically important isoprenoid-containing vitamins,'' depicting vitamin A, vitamin E, vitamin K, and vitamin D3, all of which are lipid-soluble and can be stored in body fat. (Choice A) Vitamin A is incorrect: it appears among the ''isoprenoid-containing vitamins,'' making it fat-soluble and storable in lipid tissue rather than a vitamin that must be regularly replenished. (Choice B) Vitamin D is incorrect: vitamin D3 is shown among the isoprenoid-containing (fat-soluble) vitamins, so it is retained in the body''s lipid stores rather than readily excreted. (Choice C) is correct: ascorbic acid is explicitly described as ''a water-soluble vitamin,'' so it is not retained in fat and must be consumed regularly. (Choice D) Vitamin K is incorrect: it is listed among the isoprenoid-containing vitamins, making it fat-soluble and storable rather than a water-soluble vitamin requiring frequent replenishment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the textbook''s classification of vitamins by solubility (ascorbic acid as water-soluble versus the isoprenoid-derived vitamins A, D, E, and K as fat-soluble) to a clinical scenario and infer which vitamin''s chemistry forces regular dietary replenishment.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
