-- Q5 [Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins', 'A patient with a poor diet develops an enzyme deficiency because one of their enzymes lacks a small organic coenzyme it normally requires for catalysis. A biochemist explaining the deficiency would most likely point out that coenzymes of this kind are typically derived from which dietary component?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Vitamins"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Metal ions"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Structural carbohydrates"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Membrane phospholipids"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because coenzymes are small organic cofactors that the body usually cannot make from scratch and instead obtains from dietary vitamins. LibreTexts states that cofactors "can be divided into two major categories: metals and coenzymes" and that "Coenzymes are small organic molecules that are often derived from vitamins." The text further notes that "Many biological cofactors are vitamin B derivatives" and that "Many vitamin deficiencies cause disease states due to the inactivity of apoenzymes that can not function without the correctly bound coenzyme," which is exactly the situation described in the stem. (Choice A) is correct: coenzymes are organic molecules typically derived from vitamins, so a vitamin-poor diet can leave an enzyme without its required coenzyme. (Choice B) is wrong because metal ions are the other, separate category of cofactor, not the source of coenzymes; the text lists metals (iron, magnesium, zinc, etc.) as distinct from coenzymes. (Choice C) is wrong because the textbook describes coenzymes as derived from vitamins, not from structural carbohydrates, which are not named as cofactor precursors. (Choice D) is wrong because membrane phospholipids are not identified as a source of coenzymes anywhere in the text; coenzymes are the vitamin-derived class of organic cofactors. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that coenzymes are organic cofactors typically derived from dietary vitamins.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
