-- Q8 [Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Cofactors, Coenzymes & Vitamins', 'During purification of an enzyme, a researcher finds that a bound organic cofactor remains associated with the protein through extensive dialysis and size-exclusion chromatography rather than washing away. In a single-turnover assay the purified enzyme converts one equivalent of substrate to product and then stalls, and activity resumes only after a separate reaction restores the cofactor to its original chemical state. Which interpretation of this cofactor is best supported by these observations?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A loosely bound coenzyme that dissociates from the active site after each turnover and is replaced by a fresh molecule from solution"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A metal-ion cofactor that assists catalysis without undergoing any chemical change during the reaction"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A coenzyme so loosely associated that it needs no regeneration and supports continuous turnover once bound"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A tightly bound prosthetic group whose original state must be regenerated by an additional reaction before catalysis can continue"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because the cofactor co-purifies with the enzyme through dialysis and size-exclusion chromatography, meaning it does not dissociate, and catalysis stalls after one turnover until a separate reaction restores the cofactor to its starting state. LibreTexts states that coenzymes "may be tightly bound and cannot dissociate easily from the enzyme," and that in this case "the enzyme would no longer be able to use the cofactor in another round of catalysis until the initial state of the cofactor is reformed, which takes another chemical reaction and often an additional substrate." The same passage notes that "tight-binding coenzymes are referred to as prosthetic groups," so the non-dissociating, regeneration-dependent behavior described is the signature of a prosthetic group. (Choice A) is incorrect: a loosely bound coenzyme is one that can "bind loosely with the enzyme and be released from the active site," so it would wash out during dialysis and chromatography rather than co-purify with the enzyme as observed. (Choice B) is incorrect: the cofactor here is organic and is chemically altered during turnover, since the "initial state of the cofactor" must be "reformed" before reuse; a metal that assists "without undergoing any chemical change" would not require regeneration to restore activity. (Choice C) is incorrect: this reverses the observation, because a coenzyme that is "released from the active site" and needs no regeneration would not stall after a single turnover, whereas the enzyme described clearly halts until its cofactor is reformed. (Choice D) is correct: a tightly bound coenzyme that "cannot dissociate easily from the enzyme" is a prosthetic group, and after participating it leaves the enzyme unable to act again "until the initial state of the cofactor is reformed," exactly matching the co-purification plus single-turnover-then-regeneration pattern. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between loosely bound coenzymes and tightly bound prosthetic groups to interpret the co-purification and single-turnover data and infer that the cofactor is a prosthetic group requiring regeneration.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
