-- Q9 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'A biochemist measures the initial velocity of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction while progressively increasing the substrate concentration \([S]\). At very high \([S]\), essentially every active site is occupied and the initial velocity stops increasing, leveling off at a maximum, plateau value. Which kinetic parameter is the name for this maximum, saturating velocity?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(V_{max}\\), the maximum velocity"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(K_m\\), the Michaelis constant"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(k_{cat}\\), the turnover number"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(K_{eq}\\), the equilibrium constant"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because the maximum, saturating initial velocity reached when the enzyme is fully occupied with substrate is named \(V_{max}\), the maximum velocity. The LibreTexts text defines \(V_{max}\) directly as the ''maximum velocity'' (line 26793: ''Vmax (maximum velocity)'') and states the condition under which it is reached: ''v = Vmax (or VM) when S is much greater than Ks'' (line 26943), i.e., when substrate concentration is high enough that ''saturation'' is achieved (lines 26950-26951: ''only if S >> KS ... will saturation be achieved''). (Choice A) is correct: \(V_{max}\) is the maximum velocity reached at saturating substrate concentration, exactly the plateau value described in the stem. (Choice B) is wrong: \(K_m\) is the Michaelis constant, ''operationally defined as the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity is half of VM'' (line 27363) — it is a substrate concentration (units of molarity), not the maximum velocity. (Choice C) is wrong: \(k_{cat}\) is the catalytic rate constant ''often called the turnover number ... a measure of how many bound substrate molecules "turnover" or form product in 1 second'' (line 27366), a first-order rate constant (units of s\(^{-1}\)) related to \(V_{max}\) by \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E_0]\), not the velocity itself. (Choice D) is wrong: \(K_{eq}\) is the equilibrium constant, a thermodynamic quantity the enzyme does not change (''the equilibrium constant, Keq, is unchanged'', line 26827); it describes the position of equilibrium, not the saturating reaction velocity. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of \(V_{max}\) as the maximum, saturating velocity of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 40)
  RETURNING id
)
