-- Q10 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'A researcher measures the initial velocity (\(v_0\)) of a single-active-site enzyme that obeys Michaelis-Menten kinetics across a wide range of substrate concentrations and plots \(v_0\) against \([S]\). The resulting curve rises steeply at low \([S]\) and then gradually levels off, approaching \(V_{max}\) at high \([S]\). Which shape best describes this \(v_0\)-versus-\([S]\) curve?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A straight line passing through the origin"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A rectangular hyperbola"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A sigmoidal (S-shaped) curve"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A parabola"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because a plot of initial velocity versus substrate concentration for a simple Michaelis-Menten enzyme is a rectangular hyperbola. The reference states, regarding the Henri-Michaelis-Menten equation, that ''It is a hyperbola just like the graph for the binding of a ligand to a macromolecule with a given dissociation constant, KD'' (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 26920-26921). It further specifies that under initial-rate conditions ''a plot of v vs. S is hyperbolic'' (line 26935), and the learning goals describe generating ''hyperbolic v vs. [S] plots'' (lines 26796-26797). The curve rises steeply at low \([S]\) and asymptotically approaches \(V_{max}\), which is the defining shape of a hyperbola rather than any curve that grows without bound. (Choice A) is incorrect: velocity is only ''a linear function of S when S<<Ks'' (line 26942), i.e., a straight line approximates the curve at very low substrate concentrations only; over the full range the velocity saturates at \(V_{max}\), so an overall straight line through the origin would never plateau. (Choice B) is correct: the v-vs-[S] relationship for a single-active-site Michaelis-Menten enzyme is a rectangular hyperbola, steeply rising then leveling off toward \(V_{max}\) (lines 26920-26921, 26935). (Choice C) is incorrect: a sigmoidal (S-shaped) curve is characteristic of cooperative or allosteric enzymes, not simple Michaelis-Menten enzymes; the reference notes that ''In Homotropic or Type I allosterism, binding or kinetic curves show sigmoidal fractional saturation (or kinetic curves) with increasing substrate concentrations'' (lines 21234-21235). (Choice D) is incorrect: a parabola continues to curve upward without bound and has no plateau, whereas the Michaelis-Menten curve saturates and approaches \(V_{max}\) at high \([S]\) (lines 26935, 26943), so it cannot be a parabola. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the characteristic hyperbolic shape of the Michaelis-Menten initial-velocity-versus-substrate-concentration curve and distinguish it from the sigmoidal curve of cooperative enzymes.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 45)
  RETURNING id
)
