-- Q13 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'Two enzymes, E1 and E2, catalyze the conversion of the same substrate and follow Michaelis-Menten kinetics. Under identical assay conditions, E1 has \(K_m = 0.1\ \text{mM}\) and \(V_{max} = 20\ \mu\text{M·s}^{-1}\), whereas E2 has \(K_m = 5\ \text{mM}\) and \(V_{max} = 80\ \mu\text{M·s}^{-1}\). Which enzyme most likely binds this substrate with the higher affinity?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "E1, because its lower \\(K_m\\) means it reaches half-maximal velocity at a lower substrate concentration"}, {"label": "B", "text": "E2, because its higher \\(V_{max}\\) means it converts substrate to product more rapidly"}, {"label": "C", "text": "E2, because its higher \\(K_m\\) means it remains active over a wider range of substrate concentrations"}, {"label": "D", "text": "E1, because its lower \\(V_{max}\\) means each active site holds substrate longer before releasing product"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because \(K_m\) is the substrate concentration at which an enzyme reaches half of \(V_{max}\), and it behaves as an "effective" dissociation constant for the enzyme-substrate pair: the textbook defines \(K_m\) as "the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity is half of VM" and notes it "can be thought of as an ''effective'' (but not actual) KD." Because a dissociation constant is an inverse measure of binding strength—"lower KD values indicate tighter binding"—the enzyme with the smaller \(K_m\) (E1, \(0.1\ \text{mM}\)) reaches half-saturation at a much lower substrate concentration and therefore binds the substrate more tightly than E2 (\(5\ \text{mM}\)). \(V_{max}\) is governed by a different relationship, \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), so it reflects catalytic turnover and enzyme amount, not how tightly the substrate is bound. (Choice A) is correct: E1''s lower \(K_m\) means it is half-saturated at a lower \([S]\), the hallmark of higher substrate affinity (an "effective" lower \(K_D\)). (Choice B) is wrong: E2''s higher \(V_{max}\) reflects faster turnover (\(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\)), which measures catalytic throughput, not binding affinity; a faster enzyme can still bind its substrate weakly. (Choice C) is wrong: a higher \(K_m\) signals weaker, not stronger, binding (it takes more substrate to reach half-saturation), so E2 has the lower affinity—this reverses the correct relationship. (Choice D) is wrong: \(V_{max}\) is set by \(k_{cat}[E]_0\) and does not describe how long substrate is retained or how tightly it binds, so a lower \(V_{max}\) does not imply higher affinity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of \(K_m\) as an effective dissociation constant, distinguish it from \(V_{max}\) (\(k_{cat}[E]_0\)), and reason from two enzymes'' kinetic parameters to compare their substrate affinities.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
