-- Q14 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'A purified enzyme is assayed at a substrate concentration far above its \(K_m\), where the initial velocity has plateaued at \(V_{max} = 8 \times 10^{-4}\ \text{M·s}^{-1}\). The assay contains a total enzyme concentration of \(2 \times 10^{-9}\ \text{M}\), and the enzyme''s \(K_m\) for this substrate is \(4 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{M}\). Based on these data, the turnover number (\(k_{cat}\)) of the enzyme is most likely which of the following?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(200\\ \\text{s}^{-1}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(4 \\times 10^{5}\\ \\text{s}^{-1}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(8 \\times 10^{-4}\\ \\text{s}^{-1}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(1.6 \\times 10^{-12}\\ \\text{s}^{-1}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because at saturating substrate the velocity reaches its maximum, and the textbook states that "when S >> KM, v = VM = k3E0" (LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 27089), where \(k_3\) is the catalytic rate constant \(k_{cat}\), "often called the turnover number, because it describes how many molecules of S ''turn over'' to product per second" (lines 27090-27091, 27366-27367). Rearranging \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) gives \(k_{cat} = V_{max}/[E]_0 = (8 \times 10^{-4}\ \text{M·s}^{-1}) / (2 \times 10^{-9}\ \text{M}) = 4 \times 10^{5}\ \text{s}^{-1}\), and the \(K_m\) value is irrelevant to this calculation. (Choice A) is wrong because \(200\ \text{s}^{-1}\) comes from dividing \(V_{max}\) by \(K_m\) (\(8 \times 10^{-4} / 4 \times 10^{-6}\)); \(k_{cat}\) is normalized by enzyme concentration, not by \(K_m\), and the textbook reserves the \(K_m\) combination for the low-substrate regime where "when S << KM, v = VMS/KM = k3E0S/KM" (line 27093). (Choice B) is correct: \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), so \(k_{cat} = V_{max}/[E]_0 = 4 \times 10^{5}\ \text{s}^{-1}\), consistent with "v = VM = k3E0" at saturation (line 27089). (Choice C) is a partial-truth trap that reports the numeric value of \(V_{max}\) itself as the turnover number; \(V_{max}\) is a velocity that depends on enzyme concentration, whereas the turnover number is a per-enzyme first-order rate constant "with units of s-1" (line 27366) obtained only after dividing out \([E]_0\), so the maximal velocity and \(k_{cat}\) are not the same quantity. (Choice D) is wrong because \(1.6 \times 10^{-12}\) comes from multiplying \([E]_0\) by \(V_{max}\) instead of dividing, which reverses the relationship \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\) and yields a meaningless value. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the saturation relationship \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E]_0\), rearrange it, carry out a unit-aware division, and reject the partial truth that \(V_{max}\) is itself the turnover number to arrive at \(k_{cat}\).', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
