-- Q15 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'A researcher tabulates the \(K_m\) values of several different enzymes acting on their respective substrates in order to compare them. In this context, an enzyme''s \(K_m\) is most appropriately interpreted as a measure of which of the following properties?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The maximum catalytic rate the enzyme can reach at saturating substrate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The number of substrate molecules each active site converts to product per second"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The affinity of the enzyme for its substrate, with a lower value indicating tighter binding"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The total concentration of enzyme present in the reaction mixture"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because \(K_m\) reflects how tightly an enzyme binds its substrate, so it is used as a measure of the enzyme''s affinity for that substrate. The textbook states that \(K_m\) "is equal to the dissociation constant of E and S only if E, S, and ES are in rapid equilibrium" and "can be thought of as an ''effective'' (but not actual) KD in other cases" (line 27364). Because the dissociation constant "quantitatively describes binding affinity" and "serves as an inverse measure of binding strength—lower KD values indicate tighter binding" (lines 19548, 19550), a lower \(K_m\) corresponds to higher affinity of the enzyme for its substrate, which is why \(K_m\) values are tabulated to compare enzymes. (Choice A) is incorrect because the maximum catalytic rate is \(V_{max}\), the "maximum velocity" reached at saturating substrate (line 26793), which is a separate kinetic parameter from \(K_m\). (Choice B) is incorrect because the number of substrate molecules each active site converts per second is \(k_{cat}\), the "catalytic rate constant... often called the turnover number" (line 27366), not \(K_m\). (Choice C) is correct: \(K_m\) equals the dissociation constant of the enzyme-substrate complex under rapid equilibrium (line 27364), and a lower dissociation constant means tighter binding (line 19550), so \(K_m\) is read as a measure of the enzyme''s affinity for its substrate, with a lower \(K_m\) indicating higher affinity. (Choice D) is incorrect because \(K_m\) is "the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity is half of \(V_M\)" (line 27363), a property of enzyme-substrate binding that is independent of the total enzyme concentration. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that \(K_m\) corresponds to the dissociation constant of the enzyme-substrate complex and therefore reports the enzyme''s affinity for its substrate.', 'easy', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 60)
  RETURNING id
)
