-- Q16 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'An enzyme obeying Michaelis-Menten kinetics has \(K_m = 4\ \mu\text{M}\) and \(V_{max} = 200\ \mu\text{M/min}\). A researcher assays it at a fixed substrate concentration of \([S] = 12\ \mu\text{M}\). The initial velocity \(v_0\) of the reaction under these conditions is most likely closest to which value?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(100\\ \\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(200\\ \\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(50\\ \\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(150\\ \\mu\\text{M/min}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because substituting the given values into the Michaelis-Menten equation \(v_0 = V_{max}[S]/(K_m+[S])\) yields \(v_0 = (200)(12)/(4+12) = 2400/16 = 150\ \mu\text{M/min}\), which is three-quarters of \(V_{max}\). LibreTexts gives the Michaelis-Menten equation as "V0 = VM [S] / (KM + [S])" and notes that the plot of v vs. S "is a hyperbola"; because \([S] = 12\ \mu\text{M}\) is only three times \(K_m\) (not \(\gg K_m\)), the enzyme is far from saturated, so \(v_0\) lies between \(V_{max}/2\) and \(V_{max}\). The textbook specifies that "only if S >> KS (or when S is approximately 100 x > Ks) will saturation be achieved," so at \([S]=3K_m\) the fraction \([S]/(K_m+[S]) = 12/16 = 3/4\) and \(v_0 = 0.75\,V_{max} = 150\ \mu\text{M/min}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: \(100\ \mu\text{M/min}\) equals \(V_{max}/2\), but the textbook states "v = VM2, when S = KM" — half-maximal velocity occurs only at \([S]=K_m\) (here \(4\ \mu\text{M}\)), not at the \(12\ \mu\text{M}\) used in the assay. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(200\ \mu\text{M/min}\) is \(V_{max}\) itself, which is reached only "when S is much greater than Ks"; since \([S]\) is just \(3K_m\) and not \(\gg K_m\), the enzyme is not saturated and \(v_0\) cannot equal \(V_{max}\). (Choice C) is incorrect: \(50\ \mu\text{M/min}\) is \(V_{max}/4\), the result of mistakenly using the complementary fraction \(K_m/(K_m+[S]) = 4/16 = 1/4\); the Michaelis-Menten equation puts \([S]\), not \(K_m\), in the numerator, giving the fraction \(3/4\), not \(1/4\). (Choice D) is correct: plugging \([S]=12\ \mu\text{M}\), \(K_m=4\ \mu\text{M}\), and \(V_{max}=200\ \mu\text{M/min}\) into \(v_0 = V_{max}[S]/(K_m+[S])\) gives \(150\ \mu\text{M/min}\), consistent with a sub-saturating substrate concentration that is above \(K_m\) yet well below the \(\sim100\times K_m\) needed for saturation. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Michaelis-Menten equation quantitatively and recognize that a substrate concentration only a few-fold above \(K_m\) is still sub-saturating, so the velocity falls between \(V_{max}/2\) and \(V_{max}\).', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
