-- Q17 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'A student is measuring the initial velocity of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction and has already raised the substrate concentration well past the point where the enzyme is saturated, so that essentially all of the active sites are occupied. Expecting the rate to keep climbing, the student continues adding still more substrate. Assuming the enzyme amount is unchanged, the initial velocity of the reaction would most likely:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "remain at \\(V_{max}\\), showing no further increase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "keep rising in direct proportion to \\([S]\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "decline steadily below \\(V_{max}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "stay constant only because \\(K_m\\) rises with \\([S]\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because once an enzyme is saturated, every active site is already occupied, so adding more substrate cannot speed the reaction up any further. The textbook states that ''v = Vmax (or VM) when S is much greater than Ks'' (LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 26943) and that ''when S approaches infinity (i.e., S >> KM), equation 5 becomes v = k3(E0), which is also a constant called VM for maximal velocity'' (line 27069). At saturation ''all E is converted to ES... Under these conditions, ES = E0; hence v = VM'' (line 27830), meaning the velocity is zero order in substrate and holds flat at \(V_{max}\). (Choice A) is correct: at saturating \([S]\) the rate has reached its ceiling \(V_{max}\) and does not rise with additional substrate, exactly as ''v = Vmax... when S is much greater than Ks'' (line 26943). (Choice B) is wrong: direct proportionality between rate and \([S]\) holds only at LOW substrate, where ''v is a linear function of S when S<<Ks'' (line 26942); it is a partial truth that does not apply in the saturated regime. (Choice C) is wrong: increasing \([S]\) never drives the rate below \(V_{max}\); ''v = 0 when S = 0'' (line 26941), so velocity falls only as substrate is removed, not added, and at high \([S]\) the rate is constant at \(V_{max}\) (line 27069). (Choice D) is wrong: \(K_m\) is a fixed constant of the enzyme, ''(k2 + k3)/k1 is a constant... replaced by KM'' (line 27065), and is not raised by adding substrate; the rate plateaus because active sites are saturated, not because \(K_m\) changes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the saturation behavior of the Michaelis-Menten model to predict that initial velocity caps at \(V_{max}\) rather than continuing to climb with added substrate.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
