-- Q18 [Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Michaelis-Menten Kinetics (Vmax, Km, Saturation)', 'An enzyme obeying Michaelis-Menten kinetics is assayed across a wide span of substrate concentrations, from well below its \(K_m\) up to concentrations roughly 100 times \(K_m\). If a researcher tracks how the initial velocity \(v\) changes as \([S]\) is raised through this entire range, which description most accurately captures the relationship between \(v\) and \([S]\)?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(v\\) is essentially constant at \\(V_{max}\\) when \\([S] \\ll K_m\\) and becomes directly proportional to \\([S]\\) when \\([S] \\gg K_m\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(v\\) is approximately proportional to \\([S]\\) when \\([S] \\ll K_m\\) and approaches a constant value of \\(V_{max}\\) when \\([S] \\gg K_m\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(v\\) remains directly proportional to \\([S]\\) across the entire range, increasing linearly without leveling off"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(v\\) is essentially constant at \\(V_{max}\\) across the entire range, independent of \\([S]\\) at every concentration tested"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because the Michaelis-Menten rate law has two limiting regimes that must be contrasted: at low substrate the textbook states that "v is a linear function of S when S<<Ks," so \(v\) is approximately first-order and rises in direct proportion to \([S]\) (LibreTexts 26942), while the simplified equation gives "when S << KM, v = VMS/KM" with \(k_{cat}/K_m\) behaving as "the same as a second-order rate constant" (LibreTexts 27093-27094). At high substrate the same source states "v = Vmax (or VM) when S is much greater than Ks," and that "when S >> KM, v = VM ... v is zero order with respect to S," so the velocity flattens to an essentially constant plateau at \(V_{max}\) that no longer depends on \([S]\) (LibreTexts 26943, 27089). Together these regimes make "a plot of v vs. S" a hyperbola (LibreTexts 26935, 26920). (Choice A) is wrong: it inverts the two regimes, placing the constant \(V_{max}\) plateau at low \([S]\) and the proportional rise at high \([S]\), the exact opposite of the textbook, which puts the linear dependence at S<<Ks and the constant Vmax at S much greater than Ks (LibreTexts 26942, 26943). (Choice B) is correct: \(v\) is approximately proportional to \([S]\) at low \([S]\) and approaches a constant \(V_{max}\) at high \([S]\), matching the stated low-S linear and high-S zero-order limits (LibreTexts 26942, 26943, 27089, 27093). (Choice C) is wrong: a strictly linear, never-leveling relationship ignores saturation, but the text notes that once "S >> KM, v = VM" the rate becomes "zero order with respect to S" and the curve plateaus rather than continuing to rise (LibreTexts 27089, 26943, 26950). (Choice D) is wrong: \(v\) is not constant at low \([S]\), because "v = 0 when S = 0" and "v is a linear function of S when S<<Ks," so at low substrate the velocity depends strongly on \([S]\) rather than sitting at \(V_{max}\) (LibreTexts 26941, 26942, 27093). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the Michaelis-Menten rate law to the two limiting cases of substrate concentration and reason about how the order of the reaction in \([S]\) shifts from first-order at low \([S]\) to zero-order at high \([S]\), rather than simply recalling a single definition.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 80)
  RETURNING id
)
