-- Q19 [Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)', 'Inside a cell, an enzyme is present at trace levels and encounters two competing substrates, both at concentrations far below their \(K_m\) values. To compare how efficiently the enzyme converts each substrate to product under these dilute conditions, a researcher would most appropriately compare which single kinetic parameter for the two substrates?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(K_m\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(k_{cat}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(k_{cat}/K_m\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(V_{max}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is C because \(k_{cat}/K_m\) is the kinetic parameter that measures catalytic efficiency under exactly these dilute conditions. The textbook states that "Under condition when [S] << KM, the Michaelis-Menten equation becomes v0 = (kcat/KM)[E0][S]. This really describes a biomolecular rate constant (kcat/KM), with units of M-1s-1, for the conversion of free substrate to product" (LibreTexts 27368-27369). It is also the parameter that captures discrimination between competing substrates: "The constant kcat/Km is also referred to as the specificity constant in that it describes how well an enzyme can differentiate between two different and competing substrates" (LibreTexts 27371-27377). Because the stem fixes [S] << \(K_m\) and asks how efficiently each competing substrate is turned into product, \(k_{cat}/K_m\) is the single correct comparator. (Choice A) is wrong: \(K_m\) alone is "the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity is half of VM" and reflects substrate binding/saturation, not the overall efficiency of converting free substrate to product at low [S], so it does not by itself rank catalytic efficiency (LibreTexts 27363-27364). (Choice B) is wrong: \(k_{cat}\) alone is the turnover number describing "how many bound substrate molecules ''turnover'' or form product in 1 second," which characterizes the enzyme only once substrate is already bound (the saturated, [S] >> \(K_m\) regime), not the low-[S] efficiency the question specifies (LibreTexts 27366-27367). (Choice C) is correct: at [S] << \(K_m\) the velocity is governed by \(k_{cat}/K_m\), the second-order specificity constant for conversion of free substrate to product, which is the standard measure of catalytic efficiency and of how an enzyme discriminates between competing substrates (LibreTexts 27368-27371, 27377). (Choice D) is wrong: \(V_{max}\) is not an intrinsic efficiency parameter because it scales with total enzyme, since "VM = kcatE0"; at the trace enzyme levels and sub-\(K_m\) substrate described, \(V_{max}\) does not describe the per-encounter efficiency of converting free substrate to product (LibreTexts 27225, 27366-27369). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definitions and units of the Michaelis-Menten kinetic constants and recognize that \(k_{cat}/K_m\), the specificity constant, is the parameter that quantifies catalytic efficiency and substrate discrimination when [S] is well below \(K_m\).', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
