-- Q20 [Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)] medium skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)', 'A researcher characterizing a purified, fully substrate-saturated enzyme doubles the amount of enzyme she adds to the assay while holding all other conditions constant. She wants to report the single kinetic parameter that should remain unchanged because it reflects the intrinsic catalytic power of one active site — the number of substrate molecules converted to product per active site each second — rather than how much enzyme is present. Which parameter best fits this description?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(K_m\\), the Michaelis constant"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(k_{cat}/K_m\\), the specificity constant"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(V_{max}\\), the maximum velocity"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(k_{cat}\\), the turnover number"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is D because \(k_{cat}\), the turnover number, is an intrinsic per-active-site rate constant: the textbook defines it as ''The catalytic rate constant, with units of s-1,'' that ''is often called the turnover number. It is a measure of how many bound substrate molecules "turnover" or form product in 1 second'' (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 27366–27367). Because it is a property of a single active site, doubling the total amount of enzyme does not change \(k_{cat}\); it is the total maximum velocity that scales with enzyme, since ''For all reactions, VM = kcatE0'' (line 27225). Thus \(k_{cat}\) reports catalytic power per site while \(V_{max}\) reports the throughput of the whole enzyme population. (Choice A) is wrong: \(K_m\) is ''the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity is half of VM'' (lines 27363–27364), a measure related to substrate binding/affinity, not the catalytic conversions performed per active site each second. (Choice B) is wrong: \(k_{cat}/K_m\) is the specificity constant, a second-order rate constant that ''describes how well an enzyme can differentiate between two different and competing substrates'' (lines 27368–27371); it characterizes substrate discrimination at low \([S]\), not the per-site turnover this prompt asks for. (Choice C) is wrong: \(V_{max}\) does reflect catalysis but is not intrinsic — it scales with total enzyme because ''VM = kcatE0'' (line 27225) and ''v = VM = k3E0 ... first order in E'' (lines 27089–27090), so doubling the enzyme would double \(V_{max}\), making it the very quantity the prompt excludes. (Choice D) is correct: \(k_{cat}\) is the turnover number, the molecules of substrate converted per active site per second (lines 27366–27367), and it stays constant when enzyme amount changes because the enzyme dependence lives in the separate \([E_0]\) term of \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E_0]\) (line 27225). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship \(V_{max} = k_{cat}[E_0]\) to distinguish the enzyme-concentration-independent parameter (\(k_{cat}\)) from the parameters that change with, or do not describe, the amount of enzyme present.', 'medium', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
