-- Q22 [Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)', 'Fumarase encounters fumarate (\(k_{cat} = 8 \times 10^{2}\ \text{s}^{-1}\), \(K_m = 5 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{M}\)) and malate (\(k_{cat} = 9 \times 10^{2}\ \text{s}^{-1}\), \(K_m = 2.5 \times 10^{-5}\ \text{M}\)) present together at the same concentration, well below either \(K_m\). Under these conditions, which substrate is most likely converted to product faster, and by approximately what factor?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Malate, by roughly 1.1-fold, because it has the larger \\(k_{cat}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Fumarate, by roughly 4-fold, because it has the larger \\(k_{cat}/K_m\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Neither; both are converted at equal rates because their concentrations are equal"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Cannot be determined without \\(V_{max}\\) and the total enzyme concentration"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is B because, when two substrates compete at the same concentration far below their \(K_m\) values, the ratio of their initial velocities equals the ratio of their \(k_{cat}/K_m\) values, not the ratio of their \(k_{cat}\) values. The textbook states that under the condition "when [S] << KM, the Michaelis-Menten equation becomes v0 = (kcat/KM)[E0][S]," so at the dilute concentrations described each rate is governed by \(k_{cat}/K_m\) (LibreTexts 27368-27369), and it adds that for "two competing substrates at the same concentration" the velocity ratio equals "the ratio of their kcat/KM values" (LibreTexts 28066-28067). Computing the specificity constants from the data gives \(k_{cat}/K_m \approx 1.6 \times 10^{8}\ \text{M}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1}\) for fumarate and \(\approx 3.6 \times 10^{7}\ \text{M}^{-1}\text{s}^{-1}\) for malate, a ratio of about 4.4, so fumarate is converted roughly four times faster despite malate having the slightly higher \(k_{cat}\) (LibreTexts 27442, 27444). (Choice A) is wrong: malate does have the larger turnover number, \(k_{cat}\), defined as "how many bound substrate molecules ''turnover'' or form product in 1 second," but \(k_{cat}\) governs the rate only once substrate is bound (the saturated, [S] >> \(K_m\) regime), whereas at [S] << \(K_m\) the rate is set by \(k_{cat}/K_m\), by which fumarate wins (LibreTexts 27366-27369). (Choice B) is correct: at [S] << \(K_m\) and equal substrate concentrations the velocity ratio equals the \(k_{cat}/K_m\) ratio, which favors fumarate by about fourfold (LibreTexts 27368-27369, 28066-28067, 27442, 27444). (Choice C) is wrong: equal concentrations do not produce equal rates, because at low [S] each velocity still scales with that substrate''s own \(k_{cat}/K_m\), and the two specificity constants here differ by a factor of about four (LibreTexts 27368-27369, 28066-28067). (Choice D) is wrong: although \(V_{max} = k_{cat}E_0\) does scale with total enzyme, both substrates are turned over by the same enzyme pool, so \(E_0\) cancels in the velocity ratio, and the comparison is fully determined by the \(k_{cat}/K_m\) values already given (LibreTexts 27225, 28066-28067). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the low-substrate form of the Michaelis-Menten equation to recognize that catalytic efficiency, not turnover number, governs competition at [S] << \(K_m\), then compute and compare the two \(k_{cat}/K_m\) values to predict which substrate is preferentially converted and by how much.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
