-- Q24 [Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)] hard skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Enzymes', 'Catalytic Efficiency (kcat and kcat/Km)', 'A biochemist measures the steady-state kinetics of four enzymes acting on their physiological substrates and obtains the following values: crotonase, \(k_{cat}=5.7\times10^{3}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\) and \(K_m=2\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{M}\); catalase, \(k_{cat}=4\times10^{7}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\) and \(K_m=1.1\,\mathrm{M}\); carbonic anhydrase, \(k_{cat}=1\times10^{6}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\) and \(K_m=1.2\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{M}\); acetylcholinesterase, \(k_{cat}=1.4\times10^{4}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\) and \(K_m=9\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm{M}\). At substrate concentrations well below each enzyme''s \(K_m\), which enzyme most likely converts free substrate to product most efficiently?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Crotonase"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Catalase"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Carbonic anhydrase"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Acetylcholinesterase"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The answer is A because catalytic efficiency at low substrate concentration is governed by the specificity constant \(k_{cat}/K_m\), not by \(k_{cat}\) alone, and crotonase has the largest \(k_{cat}/K_m\) of the four enzymes. The textbook states that "Under condition when [S] << KM, the Michaelis-Menten equation becomes v0 = (kcat/KM)[E0][S]. This really describes a biomolecular rate constant (kcat/KM), with units of M-1s-1, for the conversion of free substrate to product" (LibreTexts 27368-27369), and it identifies \(k_{cat}/K_m\) as the parameter that "describes how well an enzyme can differentiate between two different and competing substrates" (LibreTexts 27371-27377). Dividing \(k_{cat}\) by \(K_m\) gives crotonase \(\approx2.9\times10^{8}\), acetylcholinesterase \(\approx1.6\times10^{8}\), carbonic anhydrase \(\approx8.3\times10^{7}\), and catalase \(\approx3.6\times10^{7}\,\mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}\), so crotonase is the most efficient at sub-\(K_m\) substrate. (Choice A) is correct: crotonase has the highest \(k_{cat}/K_m\) (\(\approx2.9\times10^{8}\,\mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}\)), so under the low-[S] regime where \(v_0=(k_{cat}/K_m)[E_0][S]\) it converts free substrate to product fastest per unit enzyme and substrate (LibreTexts 27368-27369). (Choice B) is wrong: catalase has by far the largest \(k_{cat}\) (\(4\times10^{7}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\)), but its very large \(K_m\) (1.1 M) gives the smallest \(k_{cat}/K_m\) (\(\approx3.6\times10^{7}\,\mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}\)); turnover number measures only "how many bound substrate molecules ''turnover'' or form product in 1 second" and applies to the saturated regime, not low-[S] efficiency (LibreTexts 27366-27367). (Choice C) is wrong: carbonic anhydrase also has a high \(k_{cat}\) (\(10^{6}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\)) but a large \(K_m\) (\(1.2\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{M}\)), yielding \(k_{cat}/K_m\approx8.3\times10^{7}\,\mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}\), which is below crotonase''s, so a high turnover number alone does not make it the most efficient at sub-\(K_m\) substrate (LibreTexts 27368-27369). (Choice D) is wrong: acetylcholinesterase has a respectable \(k_{cat}/K_m\approx1.6\times10^{8}\,\mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}\), the second highest, but it is still smaller than crotonase''s \(\approx2.9\times10^{8}\), so it is not the most efficient under the conditions specified (LibreTexts 27368-27369). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship \(v_0=(k_{cat}/K_m)[E_0][S]\) for the [S] << \(K_m\) regime, compute and compare the specificity constant \(k_{cat}/K_m\) for each enzyme rather than ranking by \(k_{cat}\) alone, and recognize that the enzyme with the largest turnover number is not the most catalytically efficient at low substrate concentration.', 'hard', '1A', 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids', 'biochemistry', 2, 120)
  RETURNING id
)
